<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Book Is on the Table. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Book Is on the Table.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>Reading: Reading: Describe the purpose of everyday texts.<br>Reading: Outline the meaning of simple paragraphs.<br>Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>Reading: Can discover and underline or circle important words in paragraphs about classroom items (e.g., circling the word "bag" in the sentence "My bag is red.").<br>Can read and outline main points in paragraphs about classroom objects by responding to questions (e.g., "What's on the desk?" "A notebook.").<br>Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable and two-syllable words such as prepositions or objects (e.g., blending /ɔ-n/ to form the word "on" or /b-æ-g/ to form the word "bag").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays large flashcards or realia of classroom objects (book, desk, chair, pencil, marker, board). The teacher holds up each item and asks, "What is this?" Students call out the names. The teacher then quickly places items in different locations and asks, "Where is the book?" prompting "on the desk" or "in the bag".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a physical book and a physical table. The teacher places the book on the table and says clearly, "The book is ON the table." The teacher then places a pencil in a pencil case and says, "The pencil is IN the pencil case." The teacher models the pronunciation of /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "table", as well as blending phonemes for prepositions like /ɔ-n/ for "on" and /ɪ-n/ for "in". The teacher slowly points to each word in a short sentence written on the board or a large flashcard: "My red bag is here." and reads it aloud, emphasizing one-to-one word correspondence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of prepositions and object names.
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a book under the table) "Is the book on the table?" (Expected answer: No.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a marker) "Is this a pencil?" (Expected answer: No.) "What is this?" (Expected answer: A marker.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple paragraph or a set of 3-4 short, illustrated sentences describing a classroom scene. For example:<br>
                "My classroom is neat. The book is on the desk. The pencil case is next to the book. There is a colorful map on the board. My small chair is here."<br>
                (Genre: descriptive text, Register: informal, Format: short illustrated paragraph/sentences)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Locating objects and places: Using appropriate prepositions to describe familiar object places (e.g., "The book is on the table.") Confirming object locations in the classroom: "It's here." "It's on the shelf."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher displays the text. Students listen as the teacher reads it aloud. Then, the teacher asks, "What is this text about?" (Expected answer: The classroom/things in the classroom.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Questions:</b> Students read the text again, silently. The teacher asks T/F questions based on the text. "Is the book on the chair?" (False). "Is the map on the board?" (True). Students raise their hand for "True" or "False".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Circling):</b> The teacher instructs students to read the text again and circle all the classroom objects they find (e.g., book, desk, pencil case, map, board, chair). The teacher guides them by reading the first sentence and modeling circling "book" and "desk".</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Outline the meaning of simple paragraphs. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words. Grammar: WH-questions (e.g., "What's in your bag?"), Prepositions of place (e.g., on the table), Present simple (e.g., "The book is..."). Vocabulary: desk, book, marker, board, shelf, eraser, paper, chair, crayon, notebook, ruler, pencil, pencil case, pen, map; in, on, next to, between, behind.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gap-Fill Reading:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with 3-4 simple sentences about a classroom, but with missing prepositions or object nouns. Students read the sentences and fill in the blanks using a word bank (e.g., on, in, next to, book, pencil, chair). For instance: "The ____ is on the desk." "The marker is ____ the shelf." (Focus on reading the context to choose the correct word. The teacher can read the word bank aloud, blending phonemes like /ɔ-n/ for "on", /p-e-n/ for "pen".)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Scramble and Read:</b> The teacher provides sentences about classroom items in a scrambled order (e.g., "is / the / desk / on / book / the"). Students read the scrambled words, then rewrite them in the correct order to form a coherent sentence. They then read their complete sentence aloud to a partner. (e.g., "The book is on the desk.") This activity focuses on sentence structure and reading fluency.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Read and Outline Main Points:</b> The teacher provides a short paragraph (3 sentences) about a classroom. For example: "My classroom is large. There are two desks for students. One desk has a notebook and a pen. The other desk has a crayon and an eraser." Students read the paragraph silently and then answer simple WH-questions orally or by circling an option. "What is on one desk? (a notebook and a pen / a ruler and a book)." "How many desks are there?" (One / Two). This activity helps students outline the meaning of simple paragraphs.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Classroom Scavenger Hunt." Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a short written list of 3-4 classroom items and their locations (e.g., "Find the red crayon in the pencil case." "Look for the blue book on the shelf."). The text should include target vocabulary (nouns, prepositions, adjectives, possessive adjectives). Students read their list, find the items in the actual classroom or on a detailed classroom map, and circle the item once they "find" it. They confirm the location by saying, "It's here! The red crayon is in the pencil case." (Sociolinguistic: respectful interactions; Pragmatic: confirming locations).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This task directly aligns with "Discovering Our Classroom" as students actively engage with their immediate environment. It reinforces "The Book Is on the Table" by requiring them to read and understand descriptions of where objects are placed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension of instructions and object locations, collaborative interaction (reading together, finding together), and communicative task completion (successfully locating all items). Students practice silently reading instructions and then orally confirming their findings.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Classroom Scavenger Hunt" (Performance Stage) to note students' ability to read and locate items, and a short "Read and Draw" individual task. The teacher monitors for students who successfully underline or circle important words and respond to questions about object locations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Read and Draw My Desk." Students receive a blank outline of a desk. The teacher provides 3-4 written instructions on the board or a worksheet (e.g., "Draw a red book on the desk." "Draw a green pencil in the pencil case (on the desk)."). Students read each instruction silently and then draw the specified object in the correct location on their desk outline. The teacher observes how students interpret the written text and transfer it visually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Correctly draws the specified object (e.g., a book, a pencil).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Prepositions:</b> Correctly draws the object in the indicated location (e.g., on the desk, in the pencil case, next to the notebook).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Drawings are clear enough to identify the object and its location.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can read about classroom things and find them. Yes / A little / No"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I read today is _______."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to read in English today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was a little difficult for me when I read today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., The scavenger hunt effectively engaged students in reading for detail, students enjoyed circling key words.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., Some students struggled with blending phonemes in two-syllable words; more visual support for prepositions; clearer instructions for the 'Read and Draw' task.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson provided students with foundational comprehension of classroom object descriptions and locations. In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will use this understanding to actively ask and answer questions about objects in their classroom, transitioning from reading about locations to orally expressing them.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>